.TH BIFOUT 1 "October 2026" "BIDeT 4D" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
bifout \- draw a BIF (BIDeT Intermediate Format) as a PNG, GIF or SIXEL picture, or play it on a terminal
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B bifout
[\fIoptions\fR] [\fIFILE.bif\fR]
.br
.B bifin
\&... |
.B bifop
\&... |
.B bifout
[\fIoptions\fR]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B bifout
is the third tool of BIDeT 4D.  It reads a BIF, from a file or from standard input, and draws it: as a PNG, as an
animated GIF, or as SIXEL graphics on a terminal.  Line art is drawn from vectors, so it is as sharp at
.B \-\-scale 6
as at
.BR "\-\-scale 1" .
A BIF with several frames is an animation: it is written as a GIF, or, if it is going to a terminal that
reports SIXEL, played there.
.PP
The format is chosen from the name given to
.BR \-o :
.I .png
.IR ".gif" ,
.I .six
or
.IR .sixel ;
with no file name it is SIXEL if standard output is a terminal, else PNG.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BI \-o " FILE" "\fR, " \-\-output " FILE"
Write here (\fB\-\fR is standard output).
.TP
.BI \-f " FORMAT" "\fR, " \-\-format " FORMAT" "\fR, " \-s "\fR, " \-\-sixel
Force the format (\fBpng\fR, \fBgif\fR, \fBsixel\fR); \fB\-s\fR is \fB\-f sixel\fR.
.TP
.BI \-\-scale " S" "\fR, " \-\-width " PX\fR, " \-\-height " PX"
Draw at S pixels per canvas unit, or fit the picture into this size.
.TP
.BI \-\-ink " COLOR" "\fR, " \-\-paper " COLOR\fR, " \-b " auto|transparent|COLOR"
Replace the ink colour; the page colour; or choose the page for a terminal:
.B auto
asks the terminal for its background (OSC 11) and draws on that, or leaves the page transparent if it will not say.
.TP
.B \-\-frame \fIN\fR
Draw only this frame of an animation (a still).  Without it a GIF gets all the frames, and a terminal plays them.
.TP
.BI \-\-speed " F" "\fR, " \-\-fps " N\fR, " \-\-loop " N"
Animation: play F times as fast; at most N frames a second (fewer is quicker to draw and smaller); N times
(0 = until interrupted).
.TP
.BI \-\-cell " WxH"
Animation on a terminal: the size of a character cell in pixels, if the terminal does not say (default: asked, then the
window size, then 10x20).
.TP
.BR \-\-erase " " ech | bg | none
Animation on a terminal whose colour is not known (transparent frames): how an earlier frame is kept from showing through.
.B ech
(the default) erases the picture's character cells first;
.B bg
has the terminal fill unpainted pixels with its own background (SIXEL P2=0);
.B none
does neither.  See TERMINALS.
.TP
.B \-\-transparent
Leave the page transparent (a GIF with a clear colour, PNG alpha, SIXEL unpainted).
.TP
.B \-\-force\fR, \fB\-\-no\-query
Send SIXEL even if the terminal does not say that it can; do not ask the terminal anything.
.TP
.BI \-\-background " auto|transparent|COLOR"
The long form of
.BR \-b .
.TP
.BI \-\-ss " N" "\fR, " \-\-layer " ID\fR, " \-\-max\-pixels " N"
The supersampling of vector layers (default: automatic, up to 4); draw only this layer id (repeat for several); the largest
picture to draw, beyond which it is drawn smaller.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-truncated
Draw what arrived of a file that ends early.
.TP
.BR \-v ", " \-\-verbose ", " \-V ", " \-\-version
Say what was drawn, and how big; print the version.
.PP
Everything else is in
.BR "bifout \-\-help" .
.SH ANIMATION
On a SIXEL terminal an animation is drawn and encoded once, on one shared palette of 254 colours, and then replayed on a
steady clock until interrupted with Ctrl-C.  A terminal that cannot keep up skips frames; it does not slow the motion down.
A GIF is written with timings rounded from the running total to its hundredths of a second.
Into a pipe, a
.I .six
file or a PNG only frame 0 can go, and bifout says so.
.SH TERMINALS
SIXEL support differs from terminal to terminal, and animation puts it under the most strain.
.B bifout
has been tested thoroughly only in mintty, where it works well.
.B Your mileage may vary
(YMMV) in other terminals.
.PP
In Windows Terminal, which does not report its background colour, animations show noise and leftover pixels of earlier frames.
Soft shadows are not drawn there, since the page is unknown.
.B \-\-erase bg
was tried and is worse, not better.  Other terminals (WezTerm, foot, xterm \-ti vt340, mlterm, iTerm2 ...) have not been tried
by the author.  Until a terminal's own SIXEL handling improves, the dependable way to see an animation is to write it as a
GIF with
.BR "\-o FILE.gif" .
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B BIDET_NO_QUERY
If set, never ask the terminal anything.
.TP
.B BIDET_CELL
The character cell, as WxH in pixels, for an animation (see \fB\-\-cell\fR).
.TP
.B LSIX_FORCE_SIXEL_SUPPORT
If set, send SIXEL even if the terminal does not say it can.
.TP
.B COLORFGBG
Read, when the terminal will not say its colours, to guess whether it is light or dark.
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR bidet (1),
.BR bifin (1),
.BR bifop (1).
The format is described in BIF\-SPEC.md, and the whole design in README.md, in the share/doc/bidet4d directory.
